<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<task id="018" project="spaarkeai-compose-r8">
  <metadata>
    <title>FR-S08 — Remove the 4 MB first-save cap and the ~22 MB body ceiling; honest oversize pre-flight</title>
    <phase>1 Track S — Save Reliability (P0, ships alone)</phase>
    <gate>startable</gate>
    <status>pending</status>
    <rigor>FULL</rigor>
    <rigor-reason>Changes the upload path for every create-on-save; touches Graph upload plumbing and request-body limits; a wrong chunk boundary corrupts documents.</rigor-reason>
    <model-tier>sonnet</model-tier>
    <model-tier-reason>Fully specified: the chunked path already exists in-repo and is named; this is routing plus a limit change, not a design.</model-tier-reason>
    <effort>xhigh</effort>
    <parallel-group>none</parallel-group>
    <parallel-safe>false</parallel-safe>
    <parallel-reason>Touches ComposeService + UploadSessionManager, shared with other BFF worktrees.</parallel-reason>
    <deps>none</deps>
    <tags>bff-api, compose, save-path, spe</tags>
    <estimated-effort>4-6 hours</estimated-effort>
  </metadata>

  <prompt>
    Two undocumented walls block real legal documents:
    (1) FIRST SAVE dies at 4 MB — ComposeService's create-on-save calls UploadSmallAsUserAsync, which throws
        ArgumentException above 4 MB, surfaced as a raw HTTP 400. This is a METHOD-SELECTION threshold, not a
        platform limit: Graph does simple PUT to 250 MB and upload sessions far beyond. **We already implement
        chunked upload** — SpeAdminGraphService routes ≥4 MB to LargeFileUploadTask (5 MB chunks) and
        ContainerItemEndpoints auto-routes. Compose simply never calls it.
    (2) SUBSEQUENT SAVES die at ~22 MB — the client base64-encodes the document into the JSON body (~33%
        inflation) and there is no MaxRequestBodySize override anywhere in the BFF, so Kestrel's 30 MB default
        applies and fails as an opaque 413.

    Route Compose to the existing chunked path, address the body ceiling, and make oversize an honest
    pre-flight message rather than a raw 400/413.
  </prompt>

  <role>SPAARKE platform developer. Expert in the SpeFileStore facade, Graph upload sessions, and Kestrel request limits. Follow ADR-007 strictly — no Graph SDK types above the facade.</role>

  <goal>
    A create-on-save above 4 MB succeeds via the existing chunked upload path. A large replace-save is not
    silently capped by a Kestrel default. A genuinely oversize document gets a stated limit and a clear message,
    never a raw 400 or 413.
  </goal>

  <context>
    <background>
      design.md §3 S-8, verified 2026-08-19. The asymmetry matters: the replace path has no Graph-layer guard at
      all, so its ceiling is invisible until hit. Owner flagged 4 MB as "much too small a file" — exhibit-laden
      legal documents routinely exceed it.
    </background>
    <relevant-files>
      <file role="primary-edit">src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Services/Compose/ComposeService.cs</file>
      <file role="reference-impl">src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Infrastructure/Graph/SpeAdminGraphService.cs</file>
      <file role="canonical-reference">src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Infrastructure/Graph/UploadSessionManager.cs</file>
      <file role="canonical-reference">src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Api/ContainerItemEndpoints.cs</file>
      <file role="primary-edit">src/client/shared/Spaarke.Compose.Components/src/widgets/ComposeWorkspace.tsx</file>
    </relevant-files>
  </context>

  <constraints>
    <constraint source="ADR-007">MUST route through the SpeFileStore facade. MUST NOT let Microsoft.Graph types leak above Infrastructure. Scope: the Compose upload call sites.</constraint>
    <constraint source="project">MUST REUSE the existing chunked-upload implementation (SpeAdminGraphService's LargeFileUploadTask routing). MUST NOT write a second chunked uploader (root §11).</constraint>
    <constraint source="project">Any MaxRequestBodySize change MUST be scoped to the Compose save endpoint(s), not raised globally for the whole BFF.</constraint>
    <constraint source="project">MUST state the resulting supported document-size limit explicitly, and MUST make the pre-flight message name that number. An honest limit beats an unbounded promise.</constraint>
    <constraint source="project">Consider whether base64-in-JSON should be replaced by a binary upload for the save path; if deferred, record the rationale and the resulting ceiling.</constraint>
    <constraint source="bff-extensions">Placement Justification in the PR; publish size vs 44.96 MB; no new NuGet.</constraint>
  </constraints>

  <knowledge>
    <topic>SPE upload, Graph upload sessions, Kestrel limits</topic>
    <files>
      <file>.claude/adr/ADR-007-spe-storage-seam-minimalism.md</file>
      <file>.claude/constraints/bff-extensions.md</file>
      <file>projects/spaarkeai-compose-r8/spec.md</file>
      <file>projects/spaarkeai-compose-r8/CLAUDE.md</file>
    </files>
  </knowledge>

  <steps mode="directional">
    <step order="0" name="Rigor declaration">Declare rigor FULL; load spec.md, CLAUDE.md, ADR-007.</step>
    <step order="1">Read SpeAdminGraphService's chunked routing (~:2824) and ContainerItemEndpoints' auto-routing (~:156) to understand the existing contract exactly.</step>
    <step order="2">Route Compose's create-on-save through size-based selection instead of calling UploadSmallAsUserAsync unconditionally.</step>
    <step order="3">Measure the real request-body ceiling for the replace path; scope a MaxRequestBodySize adjustment to the Compose save endpoint(s), or replace base64-in-JSON with binary — record the decision.</step>
    <step order="4">Add an honest pre-flight: if the document exceeds the supported limit, the user gets a message naming the limit BEFORE the upload attempt.</step>
    <step order="5">Map the oversize failure to the correct save-outcome member (task 016) — `refused-invalid`, not a raw 400.</step>
    <step order="6">Seam-test a &gt;4 MB create-on-save and a large replace-save end to end.</step>
  </steps>

  <escalation>
    <trigger>If chunked upload cannot be reached without leaking Graph types above SpeFileStore (ADR-007), stop and surface the facade gap rather than widening the facade ad hoc.</trigger>
  </escalation>

  <outputs>
    <output type="code">src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Services/Compose/ComposeService.cs</output>
    <output type="code">src/client/shared/Spaarke.Compose.Components/src/widgets/ComposeWorkspace.tsx</output>
    <output type="test">tests/integration/seam/Compose/ComposeLargeDocumentSeamTests.cs</output>
    <output type="doc">projects/spaarkeai-compose-r8/notes/document-size-limits.md</output>
  </outputs>

  <acceptance-criteria>
    <criterion>A create-on-save of a document &gt;4 MB SUCCEEDS via the existing chunked path — proven by seam test.</criterion>
    <criterion>A large replace-save is not capped by an unstated Kestrel default; the ceiling is deliberate and documented.</criterion>
    <criterion>An oversize document produces an honest pre-flight message naming the limit — never a raw 400 or 413.</criterion>
    <criterion>The oversize failure maps to a named save-outcome member (task 016).</criterion>
    <criterion>NEGATIVE: no second chunked-upload implementation was written — the existing one is reused (verified by grep).</criterion>
    <criterion>NEGATIVE: no Microsoft.Graph type leaked above the SpeFileStore facade (ADR-007).</criterion>
    <criterion>NEGATIVE: MaxRequestBodySize was NOT raised globally for the whole BFF.</criterion>
    <criterion>NEGATIVE: a small (&lt;4 MB) save still takes the simple path and is not regressed.</criterion>
    <criterion>Publish size reported vs 44.96 MB; no new NuGet; Placement Justification in the PR.</criterion>
  </acceptance-criteria>
</task>
